(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-host-defenses-nonspecific-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Host Defenses: Nonspecific Mechanisms — Quiz 2","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which cytokine dilates blood vessels to produce heat, swelling, redness and pain?","choices":["Interleukin 6","CXCL8","Interferon gamma","Tumour necrosis factor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — increasing blood volume in the infected area. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is an inflammasome, and what does it activate?","choices":["A granule inside the neutrophil","A protein complex that activates interleukin 1 beta","A receptor that detects viral RNA","A complement convertase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and in large quantities. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What three things must the recognition system distinguish between?","choices":["Healthy self, non-self, and ALTERED self","Bacteria, viruses and fungi","Extracellular, intracellular and surface pathogens","Antigen, antibody and complement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — altered self covers virus-infected and cancerous cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which cytokine drives the acute-phase response, and which cells release it?","choices":["Tumour necrosis factor alpha, released by mast cells","Interleukin 6, released by macrophages","Interferon gamma, released by T cells","Interleukin 1 beta, released by neutrophils"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — bacteria induce macrophages to make it, and it acts on liver cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which of these is a BENEFICIAL effect of fever?","choices":["It increases production and activity of neutrophils","It increases viral replication","It suppresses T cell proliferation","It reduces tissue resistance to tumour necrosis factor alpha"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside enhanced T cell proliferation and immune signalling. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which cells make the SHORT pentraxins, and which two are named?","choices":["Fibroblasts — interferon beta","Hepatocytes","Neutrophils — defensins and lysozyme","T cells — interferon gamma and interleukin 2"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The long pentraxins come from myeloid, endothelial and epithelial cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Host defense divides into which three categories?","choices":["Barriers, fixed innate mechanisms","Skin, blood, and lymph","Bacterial, viral, and parasitic defenses","Humoral, cellular, and chemical"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — physical and chemical barriers, hard-wired innate mechanisms, then adaptive immunity. The adaptive response. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which are the PRIMARY lymphoid organs, and what does each make?","choices":["Peyer's patches and the appendix","Thymus for T cells, bone marrow for B cells","Spleen for T cells, lymph nodes for B cells","Bone marrow for T cells, thymus for B cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — primary organs produce the adaptive cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"How does urine flow act as a barrier, beyond flushing?","choices":["Osmolality and pH","It contains keratinase","It contains mucins","It is rich in complement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — mechanical flush plus two chemical properties. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What can mutations in inflammasome genes cause?","choices":["Complement deficiency","Chronic granulomatous disease","Autoinflammatory diseases","Severe combined immunodeficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which integrin pairing holds the leukocyte firmly to the endothelium?","choices":["CR1 to C3b","CD56 to MHC class I","LFA-1 to ICAM-1","L-selectin to CD34"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an immunoglobulin-like molecule on the endothelial side. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which line of defense is the SPECIFIC one, and what does it create?","choices":["The third — it creates immunological memory","The first — it creates a physical seal","The second — it creates inflammation","All three are specific"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acquired on exposure to antigen, producing antibodies or defensive cell lines. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What do endothelial cells do in response to inflammatory cytokines?","choices":["Tighten their junctions to seal the vessel","Begin phagocytosis","Secrete antibody","Upregulate adhesion molecules"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both are needed to let leukocytes leave the vessel. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What percentage are LYMPHOCYTES, and which of them belong to INNATE immunity?","choices":["55 to 90 per cent; the T cells","3 to 7 per cent; all of them","20 to 35 per cent","20 to 35 per cent; the B cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — B and T cells are adaptive. The non-B non-T lymphocytes, including natural killer cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What percentage are BASOPHILS, and what is their function?","choices":["1 to 3 per cent; they destroy eukaryotic pathogens","20 to 35 per cent; they produce antibody","3 to 7 per cent; they are the largest white cells","0.5 per cent; they release potent chemical mediators"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — constricted nuclei with dark blue granules. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is a myeloid dendritic cell's job?","choices":["Form the ciliary escalator","Patrol tissues, take up antigen","Kill virus-infected cells directly","Produce antibody"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the bridge to an adaptive response. Transport it to the nearest lymph node for T cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What is the ONE requirement of natural killer cell receptors?","choices":["They must recognise a specific antigen","They must bind complement","They must be identical on every natural killer cell","They must inhibit the cell from killing healthy self-cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — killing is a balance of activating against inhibitory signals. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What does a differential white count actually measure?","choices":["The rate of phagocytosis","The number of each type of white cell","The total white cell count alone","The haemoglobin concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — proportion is the point, not just the total. Whether they are in normal proportion. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How does fever affect tissue resistance to tumour necrosis factor alpha?","choices":["It ENHANCES resistance to its damaging effects","It reduces resistance, worsening damage","It has no effect on it","It converts it into interleukin 6"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a protective effect against the body's own mediator. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Is the induced innate response specific to the type of invader?","choices":["Only for viruses","Only after four days","No — it is still nonspecific","Yes — it is tailored to each pathogen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, though it uses more extensive pattern recognition. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How do Streptococcus pyogenes and Staphylococcus aureus evade complement?","choices":["They shed the membrane attack complex","They block the classical pathway only","They coat themselves in sialic acid","They secrete an enzyme that digests C3"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they mimic the human cell surface. Which binds factor H so their C3b is inactivated. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What do PENTRAXINS do?","choices":["Form pores in pathogen membranes","Inhibit viral replication","Cleave peptidoglycan","Target pathogens for phagocytosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bridging pathogen and phagocyte much as antibody does. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the SECOND line of defense based on?","choices":["Protective cells","Barriers at the portal of entry","Immunological memory","Vaccination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and like the first line it is nonspecific. Physiological processes and antimicrobial substances. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What can defensins kill?","choices":["Bacteria only","Non-enveloped viruses only","Only intracellular parasites","Bacteria, fungi"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the envelope is a membrane, which is what they damage. ENVELOPED viruses. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How do phagocytes limit damage from toxic oxygen species?","choices":["They stop producing them after one minute","They rely on complement to neutralise them","They synthesise enzymes such as catalase","They expel the species into the bloodstream"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — toxic species can otherwise diffuse out and harm host cells. Which degrades hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which three cytokines have systemic effects including FEVER?","choices":["Interferons alpha, beta and gamma","CXCL8, CCL2 and interleukin 12","C3a, C5a and properdin","Interleukin 1 beta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same three that act locally. Interleukin 6 and tumour necrosis factor alpha. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does NADPH oxidase produce in the respiratory burst, and what does that achieve?","choices":["Superoxide, which raises the pH","Hydrogen peroxide, which is immediately degraded by catalase","Nitric oxide, which relaxes the vessel","Lysozyme, which digests peptidoglycan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the pH shift is what enables digestion. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which cells produce interferon ALPHA?","choices":["Hepatocytes","Lymphocytes and macrophages","Fibroblasts and epithelial cells","T cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What happens if natural killer cells cannot contain an infection?","choices":["Nothing further happens","The macrophage takes over killing","They call in dendritic cells","The infection is cleared by complement alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and control passes to T cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"What is an opsonin?","choices":["An enzyme that cleaves peptidoglycan","A protein that promotes phagocytosis","A pore-forming protein","A cytokine that raises temperature"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — C3b is the example. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 48"}]};
